==English==
{{wp|<dab!>}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*peh₂-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|patroun}}, {{m|enm|patrone}}, from {{der|en|fro|patron}}, from {{der|en|la|patrōnus}}, derived from {{m|la|pater||father}}. {{doublet|en|padrone|Patronus|patroon|pattern}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈpeɪ.tɹən/}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪtɹən|s=2}}
* {{hyph|en|pa|tron}}
* {{audio|en|En-us-patron.ogg|a=US}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# One who protects or supports; a defender or advocate.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Taming of the Shrew|IV|ii|passage=patron of my life and liberty}}
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|canto=I|book=I|passage=the '''patron''' of true holiness}}
#* {{RQ:Macaulay Ancient Rome|page=154|passage=Let him who works the client wrong beware the '''patron'''’s ire!}}
## A [[guardian]] or [[intercessor]]; {{synonym of|en|patron saint|nocap=1}}.
##: {{ux|en|St. Joseph is the '''patron''' of many different places.}}
# An [[influential]], [[wealthy]] person who [[support]]ed an [[artist]], [[craftsman]], a [[scholar]] or a [[noble]].
#: {{syn|en|friend}}
# A [[customer]], as of a certain [[store]] or [[restaurant]].
#: {{ux|en|This car park is for '''patrons''' only.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=2019|author=Li Huang; James Lambert|title=Another Arrow for the Quiver: A New Methodology for Multilingual Researchers|journal=Journal of Multilingual and Multicultural Development|doi=10.1080/01434632.2019.1596115|page=4|text=In our trial of the AOT, a transect was used to collect data about the languages being spoken by '''patrons''' of the NIE cafeteria during lunchtimes.}}
# {{lb|en|historical|Roman law}} A [[protector]] of a [[dependent]], especially a [[master]] who had freed a [[slave]] but still retained some [[paternal]] rights.
# {{lb|en|UK|ecclesiastical}} One who has gift and disposition of a [[benefice]].
# {{lb|en|nautical}} A [[padrone]].
# {{lb|en|obsolete|or|historical}} A property owner, a [[landlord]], a [[master]]. {{q|Compare {{m|en|patroon}}.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1992|author=Eric O. Ayisi|title=St. Eustatius, Treasure Island of the Caribbean
|passage=[...] would obtain permission from the West India Company to settle in certain areas in the New World and cultivate the land. Sometimes absentee '''patrons''' would give the colony to a group of interested persons and the '''patrons''' would finance&nbsp;...}}

